---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import SectionHeader from "@components/SectionHeader.astro";
import CardGrid from "@components/CardGrid.astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
import { Select, Badge, Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Matchups - Replenish Guide"
    prev={{ name: "Strategic Theory", href: "/strategy" }}
    next={{ name: "Sideboard Matrix", href: "/sideboard-matrix" }}
>
    <PageHeader title="Matchups" />

        <SectionHeader title="Matchups Analysis" />
    <p
        style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); margin-bottom: 2rem;"
    >
        These are based on my results across MTGO leagues and paper events since
        the ban.
    </p>

    <div style="margin-bottom: 2rem; max-width: 300px;">
        <label style="display: block; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; font-weight: bold; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);">Filter by Matchup:</label>
        <Select
            name="matchup-filter"
            placeholder="View All"
            itemGroups={[
                {
                    items: [
                        { name: "View All", value: "all", selected: true },
                        { name: "Enchantress", value: "enchantress" },
                        { name: "Elves", value: "elves" },
                        { name: "Rock and Midrange", value: "rock_and_midrange" },
                        { name: "Sligh", value: "sligh" },
                        { name: "Landstill", value: "landstill" },
                        { name: "Psychatog", value: "psychatog" },
                        { name: "BW Control", value: "bw_control" },
                        { name: "RG Ponza", value: "rg_ponza" },
                        { name: "Moneyball (Black Aggro)", value: "moneyball" },
                        { name: "Goblins", value: "goblins" },
                        { name: "Terrageddon / Quiet Spec Oath", value: "terrageddon" },
                        { name: "Stiflenought", value: "stiflenought" },
                        { name: "Mirror", value: "mirror" },
                        { name: "Stasis", value: "stasis" },
                        { name: "Devourer", value: "devourer" },
                        { name: "Aggro with Armageddon", value: "aggro_with_armageddon" }
                    ]
                }
            ]}
        />
    </div>


    <h3><Badge theme="success">Dominant</Badge></h3>

    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="enchantress" title="Enchantress" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/enchantress/argothian_enchantress.jpg", alt: "Argothian Enchantress" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/enchantress/enchantresss_presence.jpg", alt: "Enchantress's Presence" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/enchantress/sterling_grove.jpg", alt: "Sterling Grove" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>
        The deck's best matchup. You assemble the Opal-Wave lock faster and more
        consistently than they do. If Enchantress is the best deck in the
        format, that's a great reason to play Replenish.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Take out swords and webs to bring in seal, abeyance,
        arcane denial, and maybe the 4th mage. Post-sideboard, they often bring in
        a mountain of hate (Aura of Silence, Seals, Tormod's Crypt, Naturalize). This
        makes <strong>Exalted Angel</strong> a brilliant inclusion here to completely
        sidestep their hate and beat them down while they hold dead cards.
    </p>

    </Card>
    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="elves" title="Elves" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/elves/priest_of_titania.jpg", alt: "Priest of Titania" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/elves/quirion_ranger.jpg", alt: "Quirion Ranger" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/elves/wirewood_symbiote.jpg", alt: "Wirewood Symbiote" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>
        Their whole board gets permanently exiled by Opal-Wave. Swords handles
        the key mana creatures early. They can be fast, but if you survive to
        combo they have no way back.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Take out webs usually unless you see ports. You
        can trim on meddling mage if you need more space. Bring in seal and sword
    </p>

    </Card>
    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="rock_and_midrange" title="Rock and Midrange" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/rock_and_midrange/pernicious_deed.jpg", alt: "Pernicious Deed" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/rock_and_midrange/spiritmonger.jpg", alt: "Spiritmonger" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/rock_and_midrange/cabal_therapy.jpg", alt: "Cabal Therapy" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>
        One-for-one trading can’t keep pace with Replenish refueling from the
        graveyard. They don’t have a strong clock to pressure you.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Sideboard:</strong> Frantic searches usually come out here.</p>

    </Card>
    <h3><Badge theme="info">Favorable</Badge></h3>

    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="sligh" title="Sligh" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/sligh/jackal_pup.jpg", alt: "Jackal Pup" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/sligh/fireblast.jpg", alt: "Fireblast" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/sligh/ball_lightning.jpg", alt: "Ball Lightning" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>
        They can't interact with your combo, they can only race. Name Bolt with
        Mage to keep it alive as a Jackal Pup blocker. Late game, shift to
        naming Fireblast or Ball Lightning.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Tombs out because the life loss is too much. Angels,
        Hydroblast, fourth Mage come in. Every sideboard card just needs to eat one
        of their cards. You're bridging to the combo, not trying to race them.<br
        />
        <em>Hate choices:</em>
        <strong>Chill</strong> is considered the MVP because it completely locks out
        their ability to win via a topdecked Mountain + Bolt after you've stabilized
        at low life. <strong>Sphere of Law</strong> is incredibly powerful but vulnerable
        to Naturalize and slow to cast. Avoid <strong
            >Circle of Protection: Red</strong
        >; taxing your own mana directly conflicts with your goal of executing
        your combo.
    </p>

    </Card>
    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="landstill" title="Landstill" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/landstill/standstill.jpg", alt: "Standstill" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/landstill/mishras_factory.jpg", alt: "Mishra's Factory" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/landstill/counterspell.jpg", alt: "Counterspell" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>
        Endpoint theory works here. Be patient, preserve your resources, and
        your last Replenish will resolve. I usually hard cast a few Decrees, but
        cycling Decree is an uncounterable cantrip that opponents consistently
        undervalue.
    </p>
    <p>
        Post-board, your strategy often involves overloading their mana on one
        critical turn. They usually only have enough blue sources to cast 3-4
        counterspells in a single turn cycle. You can use cheap "must-counters"
        like Orim's Chant and Abeyance to force them to tap down, then slam your
        real threats. <br /><em>Why not Mana Short?</em> While it seems perfect for
        overloading mana, it was dismissed by the community because it's too expensive
        to use defensively, it doesn't stop free counters like Foil or Thwart, and
        it lacks the utility of Abeyance (which draws a card and stops activated abilities
        like cycling).
    </p>
    <p>
        When you do jam Mage on turn 2, make sure you have a hand that backs it
        up. Look at your hand and ask what your turn 3 and turn 4 plays are. If
        there's no follow-up, wait until you have a plan for the next few turns
        before you start deploying spells. When you do play Mage, name whatever
        your hand can't already deal with. If your hand beats Counterspell, name
        their card draw. If you're setting up a combo turn and want them to
        waste time killing the Mage, name Counterspell. If you want the body to
        stick around, name Swords.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Full transformation. Frantic, Wave, Intuition,
        excess Swords out. Grid, Angels, Abeyance, Arcane Denial, fourth Mage in.
        You become a midrange threat deck. <em>Note:</em> Avoid <strong
            >Deep Analysis</strong
        > against Landstill and UW Dreadnought lists playing Fling, as the 3 life
        cost is too huge of a liability. Bringing in the 4th <strong
            >Seal of Cleansing</strong
        > is highly recommended.
    </p>

    </Card>
    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="psychatog" title="Psychatog" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/psychatog/psychatog.jpg", alt: "Psychatog" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/psychatog/upheaval.jpg", alt: "Upheaval" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/psychatog/circular_logic.jpg", alt: "Circular Logic" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>Similar to Landstill. Your threat density overwhelms their answers.</p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Full control transformation. Grid, Angels, Abeyance,
        Denial, fourth Mage in.
    </p>

    </Card>
    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="bw_control" title="BW Control" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/bw_control/vindicate.jpg", alt: "Vindicate" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/bw_control/gerrards_verdict.jpg", alt: "Gerrard's Verdict" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/bw_control/swords_to_plowshares.jpg", alt: "Swords to Plowshares" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>
        Their discard and removal can't keep up with Replenish refueling from
        the graveyard. Angel is strong post-board.
    </p>

    </Card>
    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="rg_ponza" title="RG Ponza" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/rg_ponza/stone_rain.jpg", alt: "Stone Rain" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/rg_ponza/pillage.jpg", alt: "Pillage" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/rg_ponza/rishadan_port.jpg", alt: "Rishadan Port" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>
        This matchup is heavily play/draw dependent. Your primary goal is to
        protect your mana base and ensure you hit your land drops. In fact, you
        should aggressively mulligan two-landers if your hand can't guarantee
        ongoing land drops through early disruption.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Mages come out, Angels go in. Tsabo's Web is absolutely
        essential for dealing with Rishadan Port. Even if you're running a build with
        your own Mishra's Factories, it's usually worth playing Web and disregarding
        the dyssynergy because the lock is that powerful.
    </p>
    <p>
        Be careful not to side out all your Swords to Plowshares. Trimming is
        fine, but going to zero is risky because an early Terravore can easily
        tempo you out. Some players swear by keeping Frantic Search to dig
        through Sphere of Resistance, while others find it too slow and trim it. <br
        /><em>Spicy tech:</em> Divert has seen play as a cheeky pseudo-Spell Pierce
        against their land destruction, and Sacred Ground is occasionally tested to
        completely invalidate Stone Rain and Pillage.
    </p>

    </Card>
    <h3 style="color: var(--w-color-warning); margin-top: 2rem;">
        Competitive
    </h3>

    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="moneyball" title="Moneyball (Black Aggro)" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/moneyball/wretched_anurid.jpg", alt: "Wretched Anurid" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/moneyball/grinning_demon.jpg", alt: "Grinning Demon" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/moneyball/gloom.jpg", alt: "Gloom" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>
        Newer variants running Wretched Anurid and Grinning Demon are a serious
        threat. These creatures have a high enough power level that your
        post-board Exalted Angels can't effectively solo race them. Combined
        with their infinite early discard, assembling the traditional combo is
        very difficult.
    </p>
    <p>
        <em>The MVP:</em>
        <strong>Attunement</strong> is arguably your best card here. Even if they
        turn off your graveyard with Wretch or Gloom, Attunement can single-handedly
        rebuild a fresh graveyard once their hate piece is handled. If you face heavy
        hate, just hard-casting enchantments to the board to keep Gloom off the table
        is often the winning line. <strong>Wrath of God</strong> is also fantastic
        because it can still be cast through a Gloom.
    </p>
    <p>
        <em>Fun interaction:</em> If they have a Wretched Anurid on the board and
        you manage to resolve Opalescence and Parallax Wave, you can infinitely blink
        your own enchantments. This triggers their Anurid infinitely, dealing lethal
        damage to them instantly.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Don't side out Intuition. Sacrificing all your
        velocity makes you too much of a control deck against a strategy that preys
        on control. You want to trim card disadvantage like Frantic Search instead.
        Alter Reality is a fantastic silver bullet against Gloom that you can easily
        Intuition for. Nevinyrral's Disk is also highly recommended if you have it,
        as it's a clean answer to Gloom, Dystopia, Humility, and Cursed Totem all
        at once.<br />
        <em>Typical Ins:</em> +3 Exalted Angel, +1 Seal of Cleansing, +1 Wrath of
        God, +1 Alter Reality. Keep your Swords to Plowshares to handle their big
        Demons and Anurids.
    </p>

    </Card>
    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="goblins" title="Goblins" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/goblins/goblin_lackey.jpg", alt: "Goblin Lackey" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/goblins/goblin_piledriver.jpg", alt: "Goblin Piledriver" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/goblins/goblin_ringleader.jpg", alt: "Goblin Ringleader" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>
        The textbook two-angle deck. Mage can be bad here since it doesn't block
        Lackey well on the draw and it doesn't block Piledriver at all. This
        matchup comes down to surviving the first three turns.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sideboard:</strong> All Mages and Seals out. Hydroblast, Angels, fourth
        Swords, extra Web in. Destroying Lackey turn one is the highest-impact play.
    </p>

    </Card>
    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="terrageddon" title="Terrageddon / Quiet Spec Oath" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/terrageddon/terravore.jpg", alt: "Terravore" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/terrageddon/armageddon.jpg", alt: "Armageddon" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/terrageddon/oath_of_druids.jpg", alt: "Oath of Druids" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>
        Watch for Wayfarer, Sylvan Library, and Armageddon. Having a plan for
        Armageddon doesn't necessarily mean stopping it. Not playing more than
        three lands and saving extras in your hand is a plan. Playing Angel and
        getting ahead on board so they can't profitably cast Armageddon is a
        plan.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Trim Wave, Opal, Attunement; remove all Mages.
        Angels, Seal, Denial, Web in.
    </p>

    </Card>
    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="stiflenought" title="Stiflenought" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/stiflenought/phyrexian_dreadnought.jpg", alt: "Phyrexian Dreadnought" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/stiflenought/stifle.jpg", alt: "Stifle" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/stiflenought/illusionary_mask.jpg", alt: "Illusionary Mask" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>
        How you sideboard depends on what you think their configuration is. Do
        they have Factories? Are Dreadnoughts in their post-board plan or not?
        Do they have Brain Freeze? All of that changes your approach. The
        general direction is the control transformation, but the specific cards
        you trim depend on your read. This is a matchup where knowing your
        opponent matters more than following a guide.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Grid, Angels, Abeyance in. Frantic, Intuition,
        some Waves, some Swords out. If their list runs Meddling Mage and Nimble Mongoose,
        <strong>Wrath of God</strong> is absolutely fantastic here.
    </p>

    </Card>
    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="mirror" title="Mirror" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/mirror/replenish.jpg", alt: "Replenish" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/mirror/opalescence.jpg", alt: "Opalescence" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/mirror/parallax_wave.jpg", alt: "Parallax Wave" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>
        You’re mostly just racing. Getting out a seal to protect against their
        decree is important.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Swords, Web, excess Mages, one Opal out. Grid,
        Abeyance, Seal, Denial in.
    </p>

    </Card>
    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="stasis" title="Stasis" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/stasis/stasis.jpg", alt: "Stasis" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/stasis/black_vise.jpg", alt: "Black Vise" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/stasis/forsaken_city.jpg", alt: "Forsaken City" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>Seal answers Stasis and Black Vise directly.</p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Full transformation. Swords, Intuition, Waves,
        Web, Frantic out. Grid, Angels, Abeyance, Denial, Seal in.
    </p>

    </Card>
    <h3 style="color: var(--w-color-danger); margin-top: 2rem;">Difficult</h3>

    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="devourer" title="Devourer" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/devourer/phyrexian_devourer.jpg", alt: "Phyrexian Devourer" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/devourer/sutured_ghoul.jpg", alt: "Sutured Ghoul" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/devourer/survival_of_the_fittest.jpg", alt: "Survival of the Fittest" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>
        The deck’s worst common matchup. Devourer goldfish kills faster than you
        interact, and their combo is resilient to your disruption.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sideboard:</strong> Web and Swords out. Hydroblast, Mage, Seal, Denial
        in.
    </p>

    </Card>
    <Card className="matchup-block" data-matchup="aggro_with_armageddon" title="Aggro with Armageddon" titleTag="h4" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
    <CardGrid 
        cards={[
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/terrageddon/armageddon.jpg", alt: "Armageddon" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/aggro_with_armageddon/mother_of_runes.jpg", alt: "Mother of Runes" },
            { src: "/images/cards/matchups/aggro_with_armageddon/silver_knight.jpg", alt: "Silver Knight" }
        ]} 
    />
    <p>
        Attacks life and mana simultaneously. Prioritize basics and early
        Swords.
    </p>

    </Card>
    
<script>
    const blocks = document.querySelectorAll('.matchup-block') as NodeListOf<HTMLElement>;
    const categories = document.querySelectorAll('h3') as NodeListOf<HTMLElement>;

    document.addEventListener('selectOnChange', (e: any) => {
        const payload = e.detail;
        if (payload && payload.select === 'matchup-filter') {
            const selected = payload.value;
            
            blocks.forEach((block: HTMLElement) => {
                if (selected === 'all' || block.getAttribute('data-matchup') === selected) {
                    block.style.display = 'block';
                } else {
                    block.style.display = 'none';
                }
            });

            // Hide category headers if all children are hidden
            categories.forEach((h3: HTMLElement) => {
                let nextNode = h3.nextElementSibling as HTMLElement | null;
                let hasVisibleMatchup = false;
                
                while (nextNode && !nextNode.tagName.match(/^H3$/)) {
                    if (nextNode.classList && nextNode.classList.contains('matchup-block')) {
                        if (nextNode.style.display !== 'none') {
                            hasVisibleMatchup = true;
                            break;
                        }
                    }
                    nextNode = nextNode.nextElementSibling as HTMLElement | null;
                }
                
                if (selected === 'all' || hasVisibleMatchup) {
                    h3.style.display = 'block';
                } else {
                    h3.style.display = 'none';
                }
            });
        }
    });
</script>

    <DecklistTooltip />
</Layout>

